<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meseta Central</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Inner Plateau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meseta Central is the high inland plateau that covers much of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, at an average altitude of about 600 to 700 metres. Divided by the mountains of the Central System into a northern and a southern half, it is drained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> rivers. In antiquity it was home to Celtiberian and other peoples, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it became the core of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meseta-central.jpg" alt="Meseta Central" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolás Pérez · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urue%C3%B1a_paisaje_lou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
